+## Media input admission and wire projection
+
+The existing executable Model validates inputs with the shared contracts media policy
+before calling either executor. Both `video` blocks and native `video/*` file blocks
+require declared video input support. A PDF with non-empty extracted text is a text
+input: its original MIME type does not require native PDF support and its bytes are
+not sent. A PDF with no extracted text (including an empty extraction) requires native
+PDF support. Rejection is a non-retryable local invalid-request error, before inference.
+
+The AI SDK projection independently checks whether a native video can reach the wire.
+Only the existing patched Anthropic Messages and OpenAI-compatible Chat adapters can
+serialize native video; OpenAI Responses and unknown routes reject it before the SDK
+call, even if model metadata advertises video support. Tool video files use the same
+post-tool user-media projection as video blocks rather than an unsupported inline tool
+part. Explicit `stripMedia` projection retains its existing text-only purpose; this
+does not grant media input support or change the normal executable Model admission.
+No new modality, extraction service, credential owner or provider capability is added.
+
+`packages/adapters/test/media-input.test.ts` verifies pre-executor rejection for both
+video representations, PDF extraction-to-text, missing PDF extraction, native video
+route rejection and actual SDK image/video serialization with synthetic transport.
+
 ## Verification and migration boundary
 
 `packages/adapters/test/provider-routing.test.ts` uses real SDK request construction
@@ -39,3 +61,7 @@ acceptance checks. Existing stored configuration requires no migration.
 
 Run `pnpm test:provider-routing` from the repository root. The existing pre-push gate
 also runs this suite. Desktop acceptance and paid provider tests are not part of it.
+
+## Small context compaction
+
+Keep the existing output reservation and token accounting. The default safety buffer is the smaller of 13K and 10% of the effective input window, so a small but positive window does not compact after a few tokens. Explicit buffer values (including zero) retain precedence. The 200K/1M defaults, disabled state and failure circuit breaker retain their behavior. A zero effective input budget is still a separate unresolved request-budget boundary; no artificial positive threshold is created. `packages/core/test/compact-policy.test.ts` covers the bounded change.
